yoyo --continue-strict now refuses to run when the saved session is missing or can't be parsed. It prints error: --continue-strict: could not restore <path>: <reason> and exits 1 before any model call is made. Plain --continue behaves exactly as before. All 6,185 tests pass, clippy and fmt are clean, and it's committed as 2c312c18.

What changed:
• **`src/main.rs:** the restore logic now lives in restore_session_result`, which returns either the number of restored messages or one of two errors: the file couldn't be read, or it couldn't be parsed. With the strict flag, either error prints the refusal and exits 1. This check runs before the -p branch, so the model is never called. The file path and error text are sanitized before printing. Without the flag, the warning and "no previous session found" lines print the same text as before.
• **`src/cli.rs and src/cli_config.rs:** --continue-strict turns on --continue as well as the strict check. cli.rs grew 3 lines, to 7587, so I updated its entry in tests/module_size.rs` and added a note.
• **`src/help.rs:** the flag is listed in the help text right after --continue`.

Tests:
• **Unit tests** (in a temp directory, never the repo's real `.yoyo/`): a missing file and `{ not json` each give the right error, a valid two-message session restores 2 messages, and control bytes are stripped from the refusal text.
• Process test (tests/print_stdout_contract.rs): with a corrupt session file, --continue-strict exits non-zero, mentions the flag in stderr, and the stub server receives 0 requests. The same corrupt file with plain --continue must still reach the stub and exit 0. That paired run is what stops the zero-request test from passing for the wrong reason, since every other test in that file requires a request. A comment explains the exception.
• **Positive control:** with the strict exit(1) commented out, the process test failed by name. I restored it and checked that no NEUTERED marker is left.

The docs are updated in docs/src/features/sessions.md, CHANGELOG.md (Unreleased) and the main.rs entry in ARCHITECTURE.md.

